---
import LearnLayout from "../../components/learn/LearnLayout.astro";
import LearnNav from "../../components/learn/LearnNav.astro";
import { haloCold, r9700Fn, qwen27b, flashNext } from "../../data/release041";
import { tagBlob, RELEASE_URL, RELEASE_CHANGELOG } from "../../data/learn";

const description =
  "MagnumQuant, hipfire's FWHT-rotated weight format: why a Walsh-Hadamard rotation helps 4-bit weights, the V2 group layout, and how a symmetric grid lets Flash-Next's experts run on int4 WMMA in 0.4.1.";

// Schematic only: a 16-wide group with one outlier, before and after a rotation.
const before = [0.6, -0.4, 0.3, 4.2, -0.5, 0.2, 0.7, -0.3, 0.4, -0.6, 0.1, 0.5, -0.2, 0.3, -0.7, 0.4];
const after = [1.1, -0.9, 1.3, 0.8, -1.2, 1.0, -0.7, 1.2, 0.9, -1.1, 1.0, -0.8, 1.2, -1.0, 0.9, -1.1];
const bar = (v: number, i: number, x0: number) => {
  const h = Math.abs(v) * 22;
  return { x: x0 + i * 16, y: v >= 0 ? 110 - h : 110, h };
};
const ladder = [
  { bits: 2, bytes: 72 },
  { bits: 3, bytes: 104 },
  { bits: 4, bytes: 136 },
  { bits: 5, bytes: 168 },
  { bits: 6, bytes: 200 },
];
---

<LearnLayout title="MagnumQuant and FWHT rotation" description={description}>
  <article class="distil">
    <div class="distil-meta"><a href="/learn">/learn</a> &middot; quantization &middot; FWHT</div>
    <h1 class="distil-title">MagnumQuant and FWHT rotation</h1>
    <p class="distil-lede">
      Decode speed on a GPU is mostly bytes of weights read per token, so fewer
      bits per weight means more tokens per second, until quality breaks.
      <strong>MagnumQuant (MQ)</strong> is hipfire's weight family for the
      Qwen&nbsp;3.5+ hybrid models. Its trick is a fixed rotation that makes
      weights easier to quantize, undone for free at inference.
    </p>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">01</span> Outliers waste a 4-bit grid</h2>
      <span class="lx-tag">the idea</span>
      <p>
        Group quantization stores, per group of weights, a scale and a zero
        point, and each weight as a small integer (16 levels at 4 bits). One
        large weight stretches the group's range, and every other weight in the
        group gets squeezed into a few of the 16 levels.
      </p>
      <p>
        Multiply the group by an orthogonal matrix that mixes every element with
        every other one and the outlier's energy spreads across the group. The
        range shrinks, and the 16 levels are spent where the weights are.
      </p>
      <div class="distil-fig">
        <div class="fig-caption">fig &middot; schematic: one group before and after rotation (illustrative values, not model data)</div>
        <svg class="lx-svg" viewBox="0 0 620 170" role="img" aria-label="Schematic of a weight group with one outlier before rotation and a flatter distribution after rotation">
          <line x1="20" y1="110" x2="290" y2="110" stroke="#2a2a2a" />
          <line x1="330" y1="110" x2="600" y2="110" stroke="#2a2a2a" />
          {before.map((v, i) => {
            const b = bar(v, i, 28);
            return <rect x={b.x} y={b.y} width="11" height={b.h} fill={i === 3 ? "#e11d2a" : "#6b6b6b"} />;
          })}
          {after.map((v, i) => {
            const b = bar(v, i, 338);
            return <rect x={b.x} y={b.y} width="11" height={b.h} fill="#4ade80" />;
          })}
          <text x="28" y="160" font-size="11" class="t-dim">before: range set by one outlier</text>
          <text x="338" y="160" font-size="11" class="t-dim">after R: same energy, narrow range</text>
        </svg>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">02</span> The rotation: FWHT-256</h2>
      <span class="lx-tag">how hipfire does it</span>
      <p>
        Each 256-weight group is multiplied by a fixed rotation built from a
        Walsh&ndash;Hadamard transform between two &plusmn;1 sign diagonals:
      </p>
      <pre class="prose-pre"><code>{`R = D2 · H · D1 / 16          (H: 256×256 Hadamard, D1/D2: fixed ±1 signs, 1/√256 = 1/16)

offline:     w′ = R w   →  quantize w′
inference:   x′ = R x       (the same R, not its inverse)
             y  = (w′)ᵀ x′ = wᵀ Rᵀ R x = wᵀ x      because R is orthogonal`}</code></pre>
      <ul class="distil-ul">
        <li><strong>Cheap.</strong> The Hadamard part is a butterfly: 8 stages of adds and subtracts for 256 elements, no multiplies until the 1/16 scale.</li>
        <li><strong>Free-ish at inference.</strong> The activation rotation is fused into RMSNorm (<code>fused_rmsnorm_mq_rotate</code>), so it rides on a pass the model already makes.</li>
        <li><strong>Deterministic.</strong> The sign tables come from fixed seeds (42 and 1042) shared by the quantizer and the runtime.</li>
        <li><strong>Exact in math, not in floats.</strong> The cancellation is exact in real arithmetic; in fp32 it adds rounding drift that is typically below the quantization error.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Rule of thumb from hipfire's docs: <strong>MQ for Qwen&nbsp;3.5+, HFQ for
        everything else.</strong> The rotation was calibrated against the Qwen
        3.5/3.6 hybrid weight distributions; on Llama-style dense models the math
        still cancels but there is usually no quality lift, only the extra
        rotation cost.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">03</span> The V2 group layout</h2>
      <p>
        MQ&nbsp;V2 keeps an 8-byte header per 256 weights but spends it on two
        halves: an fp16 scale and an fp16 zero for weights 0&ndash;127, and
        another pair for 128&ndash;255. The payload follows at byte 8.
      </p>
      <div class="distil-fig">
        <div class="fig-caption">fig &middot; one MQ4G256V2 group, 136 bytes for 256 weights (4.25 bits / weight)</div>
        <pre class="ascii-fig">{`  byte  0      2      4      6      8                                   136
        ┌──────┬──────┬──────┬──────┬─────────────────────────────────────┐
        │ sc₀  │ zp₀  │ sc₁  │ zp₁  │ 256 × 4-bit codes (128 bytes)       │
        └──────┴──────┴──────┴──────┴─────────────────────────────────────┘
          fp16 pair, weights 0–127    fp16 pair, weights 128–255`}</pre>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>V2 width</th>{ladder.map((l) => <th class="num">{l.bits}-bit</th>)}</tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>bytes per 256 weights</td>{ladder.map((l) => <td class="num">{l.bytes}</td>)}</tr>
            <tr><td>bits per weight</td>{ladder.map((l) => <td class="num">{((l.bytes * 8) / 256).toFixed(2)}</td>)}</tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="footnote">
        MQ2&nbsp;V2 loads and decodes but is quality-rejected as a product body;
        MQ3&ndash;MQ6 V2 are measured candidates on the product ladder. MQ4 (V2) is the default for Qwen&nbsp;3.5+ safetensors.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">04</span> Symmetric grids and int4 WMMA</h2>
      <span class="lx-tag">how hipfire does it</span>
      <p>
        An affine group decodes a code <code>q</code> as
        <code>sc&middot;q + zp</code>. If the zero point is pinned to
        <code>zp = &minus;8&middot;sc</code>, that becomes
        <code>sc&middot;(q &minus; 8)</code>: a <em>signed</em> 4-bit integer times
        a scale. Signed int4 is exactly what RDNA's integer WMMA consumes
        (<code>v_wmma_i32_16x16x16_iu4</code> on gfx11,
        <code>v_wmma_i32_16x16x32_iu4</code> on gfx12), so with activations also
        quantized to 4 bits (A4), the expert GEMM becomes an integer matrix
        multiply with one scale applied at the end, instead of dequantizing
        every weight to fp16 first.
      </p>
      <p>
        0.4.1's default Flash-Next pull (<code>{flashNext.tag}</code>, file
        <code>{flashNext.file}</code>) is a symmetric GPTQ requant: its routed
        experts are symmetric MQ4 V2, solved with Hessian GPTQ, and its trunk
        projections are MQ6 V2. At load, hipfire checks every routed expert
        header on the device for the symmetric grid; a layer takes the IU4 route
        only if every header passes. On Strix Halo and the R9700, prefill
        chunks of 512 rows or more on passing layers then run deterministic
        expert grouping, A4 activations and grouped IU4 GEMMs built with
        <a href="/learn/peacemaker">PeaceMaker</a>. Asymmetric artifacts fail
        the check and keep the F16 WMMA route; decode and MTP never take it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">05</span> What it buys you</h2>
      <div class="lx-buys">
        <div class="lx-buys-head">synthetic pp8192 prefill, 0.4.1 release defaults &middot; medians of three fresh processes</div>
        <div class="lx-stats">
          <div class="lx-stat">
            <div class="lx-stat-val accent">{haloCold.pp8192.median.toLocaleString("en-US")}<span class="unit">tok/s</span></div>
            <div class="lx-stat-label">Flash-Next, Strix Halo</div>
            <div class="lx-stat-sub">symmetric GPTQ3 artifact on the IU4 MoE route</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lx-stat">
            <div class="lx-stat-val accent">{r9700Fn.pp8192.median.toLocaleString("en-US")}<span class="unit">tok/s</span></div>
            <div class="lx-stat-label">Flash-Next, R9700</div>
            <div class="lx-stat-sub">measured on an earlier build of the 0.4.1 cycle</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lx-stat">
            <div class="lx-stat-val">{qwen27b.cards[0].pp8192.toLocaleString("en-US")}<span class="unit">tok/s</span></div>
            <div class="lx-stat-label">Qwen3.8 27B MQ4, R9700</div>
            <div class="lx-stat-sub">card A; cards C / E {qwen27b.cards[1].pp8192.toLocaleString("en-US")} / {qwen27b.cards[2].pp8192.toLocaleString("en-US")} &middot; earlier build of the 0.4.1 cycle</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="lx-buys-foot">
          Flash-Next's weights file is {(flashNext.bytes / 1e9).toFixed(1)}&nbsp;GB. Synthetic pp8192
          and prompt-file prefill are different workloads; see the
          <a href="/learn/sparse-attention">sparse-attention</a> page for long-prompt prefill.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">06</span> Limits and switches</h2>
      <div class="lx-limit">
        <div class="lx-limit-title">the IU4 route is a trade</div>
        <ul class="distil-ul">
          <li>Symmetric IU4 MoE is <strong>KLD-gated, not bit-exact</strong>: it trades increased KL divergence against the BF16 reference for prefill speed; in the release's task evals, scores tie within seed spread.</li>
          <li><code>HIPFIRE_QWEN4_MOE_SYM_IU4=0</code> restores the F16 MoE route (for quality). It changes the execution route only, not the downloaded artifact.</li>
          <li>The previous RTN-asymmetric Flash-Next file remains available as <code>qwen3.8:flash-next-rtn-asym</code>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">07</span> Sources</h2>
      <ul class="distil-ul">
        <li><a href={tagBlob("docs/QUANTIZATION.md")}><code>docs/QUANTIZATION.md</code></a>: weight families, V2 header layout, &ldquo;FWHT rotation (the M in MQ)&rdquo;.</li>
        <li><a href={tagBlob("PRIOR-ART.md")}><code>PRIOR-ART.md</code></a> &sect; 4: MagnumQuant origin and canonical files.</li>
        <li><a href={tagBlob("docs/quant-formats/mq-v2-family.md")}><code>docs/quant-formats/mq-v2-family.md</code></a>.</li>
        <li><a href={RELEASE_CHANGELOG}>CHANGELOG, v0.4.1</a> (GPTQ3 default pull, symmetric IU4 MoE route) and the <a href={RELEASE_URL}>v0.4.1 release notes</a> (numbers, opt-outs).</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <LearnNav current="/learn/magnum-quant" />
  </article>
</LearnLayout>
